  <content>
  <section><title>Equivalent Forms of Quadratics</title>
<para>Quadratics can be written in different equivalent forms:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> Standard form: \(y=ax^2+bx+c\) </item>
  <item> Factored form: \(y = (x + a)(x + b)\) </item>
  <item> Vertex form: \(y=a(x-h)^2+k\) </item>
</list>
<para>Example</para>
<para>Write \(y=(x+2)^2-9\) in standard and factored forms.</para>
<para>Here is how to find the equivalent standard form:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Expand the vertex form to multiply the binomials.
  \(y=(x+2)(x+2)-9\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the binomials.
  \(y=x^2+2x+2x+4-9\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
  \(y=x^2+4x-5\)</para>
<para>Here is how to find the factored form:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - From the standard form, find the factors of the trinomial expression \(y=x^2+4x-5\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Examine the trinomial expression.
  \(x^2+4x-5\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial.
  \((x + 5)(x - 1)\)</para>
<para>The factored form is \(y = (x + 5)(x - 1)\).</para>
<title>Try It: Equivalent Forms of Quadratics</title>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Find the equation in standard form that is equivalent to \(y=(x-2)^2+4\).</para>
  <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to find the equivalent standard form:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Expand the vertex form to multiply the binomials.
    \(y=(x-2)(x-2)+4\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the binomials.
    \(y=x^2-2x-2x+4 + 4\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
    \(y=x^2-4x+8\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
